{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#146","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#146","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#146\",\"parent_revision\":\"8ccfe11fe9907832d5bcc26d19895e5b22b2327839e60fe55abebcafbadecdf2\",\"root_revision\":\"8ccfe11fe9907832d5bcc26d19895e5b22b2327839e60fe55abebcafbadecdf2\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n#136 added a test that resolves every `python_constant` locator in `provenance-registry.json`. `opinion-catalog.json` makes the same kind of claim about the same code and remains unguarded, because it uses a different schema: free-text `references` strings rather than a structured `assertion_location`.\\n\\nNothing machine-checks those strings. A catalog row can name a symbol that does not exist, or the wrong one, and stay green forever \u2014 which is exactly the failure #136 was filed about.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence It Is Not Hypothetical\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTwo of the four broken locators originally reported in #136 turned out to live in `opinion-catalog.json`, not the registry: `HK002.valid_event_types` and `HK003.valid_hook_types` both named `HookValidator.VALID_EVENT_TYPES` / `HookValidator.VALID_HOOK_TYPES`, which are not symbols \u2014 the real constants are module-level in `hk_series.py` (`:52` and `:78`). Those two rows happen to be correct on `main` now, but nothing keeps them that way.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEither give `opinion-catalog.json` the structured `assertion_location` shape so #136's test covers it directly, or add a sibling guard that parses the `references` strings. The first is better \u2014 one locator schema, one reader, one test.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Wider Point\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSame class as #130 (catalog rows disagreeing with the enum), #124 (stubs drifting from emitters), #131, #133, and #40's registry/ErrorCode divergence: a file asserting something true about the code with nothing enforcing the claim. Every instance checked so far has been wrong when checked. Worth treating as one workstream rather than five fixes.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- #136 \u2014 the registry guard this extends\\n- #112 \u2014 the architecture spec for both files\\n- #124, #130, #131, #133 \u2014 same class\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"3d61f097f4429618832e2fb53acae7ea89f173593084b8e1a60f932723683c30\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-upw\"}"}